WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:07.212
It's 8:15pm in Afghanistan right now.

00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:10.006
And at this time, 10 days ago,

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:11.216
I was sitting

00:00:11.216 --> 00:00:12.968
in an eight-foot-by-eight-foot cell

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.219
in the basement

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:16.096
of a maximum-security prison in Kabul,

00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:18.848
where I was being held captive
by the Taliban.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.059
I'd been in that cell for 104 days,

00:00:21.101 --> 00:00:23.562
and yet, I was one of the lucky ones.

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:27.899
But I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.110
Let me go back to the beginning.

00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:33.113
This is me.

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:37.033
I'm five years old and living
in an Afghan refugee camp

00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:38.743
in Peshawar, Pakistan.

00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:43.707
You can see the two patches
on my UNHCR-donated jeans.

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:48.211
One patch is an American flag
and the other is the Statue of Liberty,

00:00:48.211 --> 00:00:52.465
the quintessential beacons
of the American dream I so badly wanted.

00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:56.761
But as a refugee,
I didn't have a state to call home.

00:00:56.761 --> 00:00:59.681
And not having a state,
I didn't have rights.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.310
And not having rights,
I didn't have a voice.

00:01:04.311 --> 00:01:06.688
I needed someone to advocate for me.

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:10.108
And I was fortunate to have two advocates.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.403
My parents, who had emigrated
to the US four years ahead of me,

00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:17.198
fought tirelessly to get me to America
for those four years.

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:19.743
They gave me a voice.

00:01:19.784 --> 00:01:21.911
They gave me the American dream.

00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:25.707
And my parents' advocacy
allowed me to join them,

00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:27.250
to emigrate to Omaha, Nebraska,

00:01:27.292 --> 00:01:28.918
to go to high school in the states

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.211
to serve in the military,

00:01:30.253 --> 00:01:33.256
to graduate as a Tillman Scholar
from Georgetown University,

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:35.634
to get accepted into medical school.

00:01:35.675 --> 00:01:37.636
I became the American dream.

00:01:37.636 --> 00:01:42.557
(Cheers and applause)

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:46.061
And in my dream,
everything came full circle.

00:01:46.061 --> 00:01:48.563
This past summer,
I deferred one dream for another

00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:50.273
when I put medical school on hold,

00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:52.150
founded Human First Coalition,

00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:56.029
an organization dedicated
to aiding Afghans in need.

00:01:57.072 --> 00:02:00.241
Having spent the first
17 years of my life stateless,

00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:02.077
I rather abruptly found myself

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:06.956
in the role of the humanitarian aid worker
who had shaped so much of my life.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.586
I became that advocate
and that voice for others.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:13.922
And becoming an aid worker,

00:02:13.922 --> 00:02:16.299
I got to provide food,
medical care and shelter

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:18.134
for thousands of Afghans.

00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:21.471
I got to operate resettlement flights
for thousands more,

00:02:21.513 --> 00:02:25.392
and I got to make multiple trips to Kabul,
with the Taliban’s blessing,

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:26.851
to oversee it all.

00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:29.896
But then, on December 18, 2021,

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:31.439
everything changed.

00:02:32.315 --> 00:02:34.984
That morning, which started off
like any other morning,

00:02:34.984 --> 00:02:37.529
I was suddenly, inexplicably
and unexpectedly

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.364
taken into Taliban custody.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:41.157
As I was being driven

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:44.327
to the Taliban’s General Directorate
of Intelligence headquarters,

00:02:44.369 --> 00:02:47.414
I was told that I just needed
to answer a few routine questions,

00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:49.708
and then I’d be sent back to my hotel.

00:02:50.208 --> 00:02:53.837
Instead, a few hours later,
I was brought into that basement room.

00:02:53.837 --> 00:02:57.382
Three roommates, no blanket,
no mattress, no pillow, no sun,

00:02:57.424 --> 00:02:59.592
no way to communicate
with the outside world

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:02.095
and no clue what was to become of me.

00:03:02.595 --> 00:03:05.223
And from that moment on,
I stayed in that basement

00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:08.893
in the dead of the Afghan
winter, for 105 days.

00:03:10.228 --> 00:03:13.898
The entire time, I feared that I might be
taken in front of a firing squad,

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.066
at any minute.

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:18.361
And on the 45th day,

00:03:18.403 --> 00:03:21.448
I was beaten and tortured
by 11 men wielding pipes,

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:23.867
after eight days on a hunger strike.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.078
I had done nothing wrong.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.498
But again, I was lucky.

00:03:30.498 --> 00:03:33.001
I not only had several
exceptional advocates,

00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:34.377
but I had a state.

00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:36.337
The US government game me a voice

00:03:36.337 --> 00:03:37.922
and asserted my human rights,

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:40.675
and that's what ultimately
led to my release

00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:41.843
10 days ago.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:48.057
(Applause)

00:03:48.391 --> 00:03:49.809
And on the day of my release,

00:03:49.851 --> 00:03:52.187
following a four-hour flight
from Kabul to Qatar,

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:57.233
the euphoria I felt as I walked off
that plane is indescribable.

00:03:58.777 --> 00:04:00.945
But amidst the triumph
and joy of homecoming,

00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:02.572
something was amiss.

00:04:03.198 --> 00:04:06.159
On the day of my release,
I returned to society

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:09.746
to find that the humanitarian crisis
in Afghanistan was only getting worse,

00:04:09.746 --> 00:04:11.664
the whole world was fixated on Ukraine,

00:04:11.664 --> 00:04:13.833
and nearly every Afghan
resettlement effort

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.711
was on pause or had ground
to a screeching halt,

00:04:16.753 --> 00:04:19.297
with no effective resolution in sight.

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:23.968
And that was a huge letdown.

00:04:24.886 --> 00:04:29.265
And that is why I decided
that before even visiting my family,

00:04:29.265 --> 00:04:31.017
I had to get back to work.

00:04:32.727 --> 00:04:35.772
Because the truth of the matter
is 95 percent of Afghans

00:04:35.772 --> 00:04:37.774
are currently not getting enough to eat.

00:04:37.774 --> 00:04:40.985
22.8 million are facing
crisis levels of hunger.

00:04:41.027 --> 00:04:45.782
97 percent are living
below the poverty line.

00:04:45.824 --> 00:04:48.660
And 9 million are living
in extreme poverty.

00:04:49.869 --> 00:04:52.580
And these are the individuals
who fought alongside us

00:04:52.622 --> 00:04:55.917
in the international coalition
for the last 20 years.

00:04:56.793 --> 00:04:58.086
They are our allies.

00:04:58.127 --> 00:04:59.796
They are our brothers and sisters,

00:04:59.838 --> 00:05:01.798
and they are in desperate straits.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.469
The magnitude of the work ahead
is staggering.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.681
But we have to start somewhere.

00:05:09.681 --> 00:05:12.559
And the question becomes
"What should we do?"

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.811
In my mind, four things.

00:05:15.353 --> 00:05:18.147
First, we must bolster
humanitarian aid provisions

00:05:18.189 --> 00:05:19.732
for the international community

00:05:19.732 --> 00:05:24.445
by increasing support for and donations
to grassroots organizations

00:05:24.445 --> 00:05:28.366
that have infrastructure to ensure
aid is getting directly into the hands

00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:30.743
of the people who need it the most.

00:05:31.703 --> 00:05:33.830
And that's not just my organization --

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.877
there are so many support-worthy groups
doing exceptional work in Afghanistan,

00:05:38.877 --> 00:05:40.628
and we need resources now.

00:05:41.838 --> 00:05:43.339
Second, we, government

00:05:43.339 --> 00:05:46.759
and citizens of the 36 countries
who fought in Afghanistan

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:48.887
must keep our promises
to our Afghan allies,

00:05:48.928 --> 00:05:50.847
to whom we owe a debt,

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:52.640
by fast-tracking their resettlement,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.310
be it in the United States or elsewhere.

00:05:55.894 --> 00:06:00.940
(Applause)

00:06:00.982 --> 00:06:03.902
This, first and foremost,
necessitates resettling refugees

00:06:03.943 --> 00:06:06.487
who are currently waiting
in camps around the world,

00:06:06.487 --> 00:06:09.157
to reach a place that they can call home.

00:06:09.198 --> 00:06:12.493
It also calls upon the international
community to increase quotas

00:06:12.535 --> 00:06:13.703
for Afghan allies

00:06:13.703 --> 00:06:16.539
to accept more at-risk Afghans
for permanent resettlement

00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:20.043
and to expand programs
like humanitarian parole,

00:06:20.043 --> 00:06:22.503
to generate more pathways
for those in need.

00:06:23.463 --> 00:06:24.797
Third ...

00:06:24.839 --> 00:06:27.216
As we find pathways forward
for these individuals,

00:06:27.216 --> 00:06:31.137
we must not separate families,
we must preserve them.

00:06:31.512 --> 00:06:35.600
(Applause)

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:37.727
Or, at the very least,
create strict limits

00:06:37.727 --> 00:06:41.105
for the amount of time that families
can be separated.

00:06:42.315 --> 00:06:45.777
Family separation like mine
generates irreparable harm.

00:06:47.403 --> 00:06:50.907
But clear and specific change
to existing admission policies

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:53.618
can ensure that other minors
do not face the same fate

00:06:53.618 --> 00:06:55.578
that befell me and my family.

00:06:56.871 --> 00:07:00.792
Fourth -- and this is the most
important of them all --

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:05.672
we must reestablish an international
diplomatic presence in Afghanistan,

00:07:05.672 --> 00:07:08.257
to hold the Taliban accountable
for their actions

00:07:08.299 --> 00:07:11.386
and provide consular services
to the people.

00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:14.222
It opens a channel
to address Taliban’s actions,

00:07:14.263 --> 00:07:15.431
rather than cutting off,

00:07:15.473 --> 00:07:18.184
isolating and eliminating
avenues for influence.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:23.356
And I've witnessed what engagement
with the Taliban can look like firsthand.

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:26.234
The negotiations that resulted
in my release from captivity

00:07:26.275 --> 00:07:29.737
were the direct result of effective
diplomacy with the Taliban

00:07:29.779 --> 00:07:31.447
on the world stage.

00:07:32.448 --> 00:07:36.244
Diplomats spoke to each other openly
and resolved an issue of mutual concern.

00:07:36.911 --> 00:07:40.039
And while the success of this discussion
is perhaps an anomaly,

00:07:40.081 --> 00:07:42.875
the kind of diplomacy
demonstrated by my release

00:07:42.875 --> 00:07:46.170
can and should serve as a model
for achieving other desired change

00:07:46.212 --> 00:07:48.172
for the future of Afghanistan,

00:07:48.172 --> 00:07:51.884
such as the restoration
of girls' education above grade six,

00:07:51.884 --> 00:07:53.970
freedom of press,
bolstering women's rights,

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.348
and most urgently,
increasing humanitarian assistance.

00:07:57.724 --> 00:07:59.058
At the same time --

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.560
(Applause)

00:08:00.601 --> 00:08:04.063
At the same time, our diplomacy
can't be a blank check.

00:08:04.105 --> 00:08:07.358
The Taliban must live up
to their end of the bargain

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.945
to demonstrate that they are ready
to engage in diplomacy,

00:08:10.987 --> 00:08:14.032
as an actor that upholds
basic human rights,

00:08:14.073 --> 00:08:16.284
that ensures necessary freedoms

00:08:16.325 --> 00:08:18.995
and that does not take or hold hostages.

00:08:19.287 --> 00:08:22.999
(Applause)

00:08:23.416 --> 00:08:24.625
At the end of the day,

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:27.837
the situation in Afghanistan
is an extremely complex one.

00:08:28.629 --> 00:08:30.757
It can't be summed up
in an eight-minute talk

00:08:30.798 --> 00:08:33.843
I wrote four days ago,
emerging from captivity.

00:08:35.178 --> 00:08:36.888
Yet there are tangible solutions,

00:08:36.888 --> 00:08:40.224
and I'm in the privileged position
of being able to advocate for them,

00:08:40.224 --> 00:08:42.769
but I'm here today to tell you
that you are too.

00:08:44.187 --> 00:08:45.605
The truth of the matter is,

00:08:45.605 --> 00:08:47.774
especially in the case of Afghanistan,

00:08:47.774 --> 00:08:51.652
change has always and will continue
to start with everyday people.

00:08:51.694 --> 00:08:54.572
This fall, tens of thousands of people
from around the world

00:08:54.614 --> 00:08:58.659
banded together at the grassroots level
to aid Afghans in need.

00:08:58.659 --> 00:09:00.787
You don't need to be an expert to engage,

00:09:00.787 --> 00:09:03.539
to volunteer, to contribute, to lobby,

00:09:03.539 --> 00:09:07.627
or even to simply welcome a refugee
to advocate for them.

00:09:08.377 --> 00:09:10.463
As Margaret Mead once said,

00:09:10.463 --> 00:09:14.175
"Never doubt that a small group
of thoughtful, committed citizens

00:09:14.217 --> 00:09:15.551
can change the world.

00:09:15.593 --> 00:09:18.387
Indeed, it's the only thing
that ever has."

00:09:20.515 --> 00:09:23.017
On the afternoon
of my release 10 days ago,

00:09:23.059 --> 00:09:26.437
I, at long last, climbed out
of my basement cell

00:09:26.479 --> 00:09:27.688
and into the sunlight,

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.733
without anything binding my hands
or covering my eyes.

00:09:31.818 --> 00:09:33.444
I could see the sky.

00:09:34.362 --> 00:09:37.532
I traveled out of the prison
through Kabul city in a Corolla sedan.

00:09:37.573 --> 00:09:39.534
I passed the American embassy

00:09:39.575 --> 00:09:41.786
and arrived at the Kabul
International Airport.

00:09:41.786 --> 00:09:45.331
I walked onto the tarmac,
I climbed into the C-17,

00:09:45.373 --> 00:09:49.335
I shook hands with American,
Qatari and British diplomats,

00:09:49.335 --> 00:09:51.838
and suddenly, I was a free man again.

00:09:53.548 --> 00:09:56.134
But again, I was one of the lucky ones.

00:09:57.343 --> 00:10:01.055
Ultimately, being a captive reminded me
of a time when I was helpless

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:02.723
and needed a voice.

00:10:02.765 --> 00:10:05.143
Now that I'm released,
I have my voice back,

00:10:05.184 --> 00:10:08.938
and, mercifully, it puts me in a position
where I can advocate

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.440
for that little boy
with the Statue of Liberty patch

00:10:11.482 --> 00:10:13.484
on his UNHCR-donated jeans,

00:10:13.526 --> 00:10:15.319
chasing the American dream.

00:10:15.945 --> 00:10:17.321
I hope you'll join me.

00:10:17.363 --> 00:10:24.370
(Applause)

